// ORDER DEPENDENCE -- read this before editing.
//
// This fixture depends on the executor's ready queue being FIFO round-robin
// (`crates/nova-runtime/src/task.rs:184`): a task re-queued by `yield_now`
// waits behind every task already waiting. That is a documented runtime
// invariant, not an observed coincidence, but it is what makes the
// interleaving below deterministic. The output order IS the assertion here,
// so an edit that reorders the two `spawn`s, or adds a suspension point to
// either task, changes what this pins.
//
// WHAT THIS PINS: `recv` does not suspend when a value is already buffered.
// Its `yield_now().await` belongs on the retry path and nowhere else, so a
// `recv` that can answer immediately must answer without yielding. Loading the
// channel before either task is spawned -- the `try_send` in `main`, NOT the
// `close` after it -- is what makes `consume`'s first `pop` succeed so that its
// retry loop is never entered at all.
//
// Measured to catch that yield moved to EITHER side of the loop: to before the
// first `pop`, and to after the loop. Both make `marker` print first, so what
// this actually pins is that there is no suspension point anywhere on the
// immediate-answer path, which is a little stronger than "in the retry path".
//
// WHY IT IS NEEDED: `channel_two_tasks_blocking` is BLIND to this. Moving that
// one `yield_now().await` out of the retry loop to before the first `pop`
// leaves it -- and every other test in the suite except this one -- green
// while making the code livelock-prone, because that fixture's interleaving
// never runs the retry-loop body twice in a row: after its entry yield the
// producer has always either pushed or closed, so `pop` succeeds or `closed`
// is set on the first look and the missing in-loop suspension is never needed.
// Measured, not assumed. Before this fixture existed that move left all 44
// targets green, which is why it was written.
//
// WHY IT PINS PLACEMENT FROM THIS SIDE: the direct failure mode of a retry
// loop with no suspension point is a livelock, not a wrong answer -- the
// consumer spins forever and, under cooperative scheduling, starves the
// producer that would have refilled the channel. A fixture asserting that
// directly could only ever hang, which would wedge CI instead of reporting.
// So this attacks the same mutation from the terminating side. Two facts
// together pin the placement: `channel_two_tasks_blocking` pins that `recv`
// waits at all (deleting the wait truncates its output and then hangs), and
// this pins that the wait is not anywhere on the immediate-answer path. A
// `recv` that waits somewhere, but not where an immediate answer would reach
// it, waits on the retry path.
async fn consume(mut rx: Receiver<Int>) {
    match rx.recv().await {
        Some(v) => println("recv ${v}")
        None => println("recv none")
    }
}

// A second runnable task, so that a suspension inside `consume` is
// observable as output order rather than as a timing difference nothing can
// assert on.
async fn marker() {
    println("marker")
}

async fn main() {
    let ch: Channel<Int> = channel(2)
    let mut tx = ch.sender()
    let sent = tx.try_send(7)
    if !sent { println("unexpected: refused") }
    // NOT dead code, and NOT what makes the first `pop` succeed -- the
    // `try_send` above does that. This `close` is the fixture's TERMINATION
    // guard and the most load-bearing line in it. Should `pop` ever regress to
    // returning `None`, `recv` reads `closed`, returns `None` and prints
    // `recv none`: a bounded diff against the golden, so this fixture FAILS.
    // Delete the `close` and the same regression makes `recv` yield forever
    // and the fixture HANGS instead. Measured both ways: `recv none` and exit
    // 0 with it, no output and a timeout kill without. For a fixture whose
    // entire justification is reporting rather than hanging, that is the point
    // of the line.
    //
    // `channel_send_suspends_only_to_retry` has NO equivalent guard and so can
    // hang: with `push` regressed to refuse everything its `send` spins
    // forever (measured, timeout kill). Closing there would not fix it -- a
    // closed channel makes `send` return false immediately and the fixture
    // would pin nothing -- and a third task to close it would add the very
    // suspension point this family of fixtures depends on the absence of.
    // Documented, not fixed.
    tx.close()
    let a = spawn(consume(ch.receiver()))
    let b = spawn(marker())
    a.join().await
    b.join().await
}
